 dislocated due to AI-driven displacement, the risk of societal fragmentation and instability escalates dramatically, impacting democratic processes and public safety far beyond economic metrics. \n\nReactive measures like unemployment benefits, job retraining programs, and basic income proposals are fundamentally inadequate for the scale and speed of AI-driven structural unemployment; instead, proactive, robust international and institutional AI governance, coupled with \"skin-in-the-game\" for AI providers, is essential to prevent these harms and safeguard human futures. Market self-regulation has historically failed to manage systemic risks, as evidenced by financial crises and environmental degradation, where the costs of externalities were borne by the public, not just the polluters. We must look to analogous global threats, such as nuclear proliferation or climate change, which demonstrate the necessity of international frameworks, treaties, and enforceable standards to manage technologies with global impact. Regulators must implement mandatory pre-deployment impact assessments that include comprehensive labor market analyses, and AI developers must contribute financially to a global fund for worker adaptation and societal resilience, ensuring they bear a proportional share of the societal costs their technologies impose. This approach is not about stifling innovation but about ensuring that progress is sustainable and equitable, built upon a foundation of shared safety and accountability.\n",
