Instead of stifling innovation with traditional 'governance (oversight)', policymakers should focus on 'Enabling Competitive Transparency Through Regulatory Scaffolding' (acc-intentions-078) to manage risks. This framework, which includes disclosure-based regulation and liability proportional to opacity, creates market incentives for 'transparency (verification)' and the open-sourcing of security-critical AI capabilities, fostering 'safety (empirical)' through collective scrutiny. This mechanism leverages competitive pressure to drive robust safety practices and accountability, rather than relying on slow, centralized mandates that create 'risk (innovation stagnation)' and fail to keep pace with AI's rapid advancements.

